// scoped_id.peg — formal grammar for the madder scoped-id text form:
// the blob-store name (scope prefix + dot-depth) plus the optional
// `@<markl-id>` digest pin (FDR-0008 Phase 2 / FDR-0010). Companion to
// go/internal/alfa/scoped_id/main.go's Id.Set decoder and to FDR-0010
// (docs/features/0010-scoped-id-resolution.md).
//
// Notation: Ford PEG, authored langlang-compatible, matching piggy's
// marklid.peg and hyphence's hyphence-content.peg conventions (// comments
// only, explicit spacing, no automatic space insertion). Validate with
// BOTH `-disable-builtins -disable-spaces` (the hyphence#7 dialect-gap
// finding: -disable-builtins alone leaves the whitespace-injector pass on,
// which auto-inserts Spacing between sequence items this grammar never
// asked for).
//
// Whitespace: a scoped-id has NO whitespace anywhere. Every NameRune and
// digest primitive excludes it, and no rule calls a Spacing production.
//
// SCOPE (grammar vs decoder): this file is STRUCTURAL only — scope shape,
// name charset, and the two-slot digest form. It does NOT and per PEG
// cannot encode: the blech32 checksum (a BCH computation), payload length,
// or markl format/purpose registration. Those stay Id.Set's job — a
// grammar-valid string can still be decoder-rejected (an unregistered
// `@uuidv7-…` format, an abbreviated digest, a bad-checksum payload). That
// two-dimension split is pinned by testdata/scoped_id_vectors.txt, whose
// `grammar` column this file governs and whose `parser` column Id.Set
// governs, exactly the piggy RFC 0011 §4.1 grammar-vs-decoder precedent.
//
// ---- imported from piggy (single source of truth) --------------------
//
// Per the 2026-07-22 grammar-composition ruling (one home per grammar
// unit, composed upstream→downstream via langlang @import), the markl-id
// PRIMITIVES are imported from piggy's marklid.peg (piggy owns the markl-id
// format, RFC 0011), not redefined here. langlang resolves @import RELATIVE
// to the importing file, so marklid.peg must be staged beside this file at
// validation time — the grammar-vectors nix gate stages it from piggy's
// `.#marklid-grammar` flake output, the same way hyphence-content.peg
// stages it.
//
//   Format, DataChar   the digest-slot primitives (piggy#237). DataChar is
//                      the blech32 alphabet; Format is the format-id rune
//                      class.
//   PurposeBare        the bare purpose production (piggy RFC 0011 §2.1).
//   String             the doddish quoting rules (piggy#236), for a quoted
//                      purpose.
@import Format, DataChar, PurposeBare, String from "./marklid.peg"

// ---- entry point -----------------------------------------------------
//
// A scoped-id is a scoped NAME optionally followed by a `@<markl-id>` pin,
// then end of input. Id.Set splits on the FIRST `@`: everything left is the
// name, everything right is parsed as a markl-id. NameRune excludes `@`, so
// the first `@` is unambiguously the name/pin boundary.
ScopedId   <- ScopedName Pin? EOF

// ---- the scoped name (scope prefix + dot-depth + name) ---------------
//
// Ordered choice mirrors Id.Set's prefix dispatch. CwdName and the slash
// forms are tried before the prefix-rune forms, which are tried before the
// bare UserName, because a leading `.`, `/`, `%`, `~`, or `_` selects a
// non-user scope (Id.Set: SetPrefix), while any other leading NameRune is
// the bare XDG-user scope. UnknownName ('_' …) precedes UserName so a
// LEADING `_` reads as the Unknown-scope prefix (Id.Set's SetPrefix('_')),
// even though `_` is itself a NameRune interior to a name (e.g. widget_1).
ScopedName <- CwdName
            / SystemName
            / CacheName
            / AliasName
            / UnknownName
            / UserName

// `.name` depth 0, `..name` depth 1, … (Id.Set stores dots-1 as cwdDepth).
// At least one dot AND a name: an all-dots string ('.', '...') has no name
// and is rejected (Id.Set: "all dots, no name").
CwdName    <- '.'+ Name

// FDR-0019 system scope. `//name` forces the system scope; `/name` is the
// remote-first spelling; a bare `/` is the legacy nameless system selector.
// `//` alone (no name) is rejected. The `//` alternative is first so it is
// not shadowed by the single-slash form.
SystemName <- '//' Name
            / '/' Name
            / '/'

// `%name` XDG cache, `~name` the parse-only XDG-user alias, `_name` the
// Unknown scope.
CacheName   <- '%' Name
AliasName   <- '~' Name
UnknownName <- '_' Name

// Bare (no prefix) is the XDG-user scope.
UserName   <- Name

// The name charset (Id.Set: validateName) — [a-zA-Z0-9_-]. Excludes `@`,
// so the pin boundary is unambiguous; excludes `.`/`/`/`%`/`~`, so a
// scope prefix is never mistaken for name interior.
Name       <- NameRune+
NameRune   <- [a-zA-Z0-9_-]

// ---- the digest pin (`@<markl-id>`) ----------------------------------
//
// The pin is a full markl-id: an optional purpose slot joined by `@` to a
// digest slot. The PIN CURRENCY is the bare `@<digest>` (no purpose); the
// purpose-ful form is grammatical (Id.Set accepts it, splitting on the
// first `@` and letting markl's quote-aware scan find the purpose-join
// `@`) but is not the currency. A purpose containing `@` is spelled quoted
// (`"a@b"@fmt-…`); the join is then the SECOND `@`.
Pin        <- '@' MarklId
MarklId    <- (Purpose '@')? DigestSlot
Purpose    <- PurposeBare / String

// The digest slot is composed from piggy's exported Format/DataChar
// primitives as `Format '-' DataChar+` — CHARSET-STRICT but LENGTH-
// AGNOSTIC, so it admits both a full digest and an ABBREVIATED one (a legal
// prefix, e.g. `blake2b256-9ft3x`); length and checksum stay Id.Set's
// concern. This is exactly the composition piggy documents on its exported
// DataChar (and that trellis/hyphence use); piggy deliberately does not
// export the composed shape, so each consumer owns its length policy. The
// trailing `!NameRune` rejects junk after the data run (`blake2b256-9bt3` —
// `b` is outside the blech32 alphabet — fails here rather than leaving a
// dangling NameRune).
DigestSlot <- Format '-' DataChar+ !NameRune

EOF        <- !.

// ---- conformance vectors ---------------------------------------------
//
// Not duplicated here — the shared corpus at
// go/internal/alfa/scoped_id/testdata/scoped_id_vectors.txt pins every
// vector's `grammar` outcome (this file, via the langlang grammar-vectors
// gate) alongside its `parser` outcome (Id.Set, via vectors_test.go). A
// third copy here would drift silently.
